\begin{abstract}
We analyse the sensitivity of a tied rank-correlation instrument --- the \emph{zero-fit dial} --- to the bit length of the integers on which it is computed. The dial's accuracy ceiling is governed by the tie profile of a $2$-adic valuation grading of the sample $\{0,\dots,2^{b}-1\}$ and by the tie profiles of the coarsened (``blinded'') responses derived from it. Our main structural result is a rigidity theorem: every ceiling of the resulting ladder --- the coarse (bare-count) ceiling at dyadic relation rate $p=2^{-t}$, the tip-blind ceiling at depth $t$, and the bulk-blind ceiling at depth $t$ --- is a one-parameter family in the single quantity $X=8^{b}$ of the affine shape $(Xg+h)/(X-1)$ with a bitlen-free $g\in[0,1]$ and $|h|\le 1$. Consequently every ceiling lies within $3/X$ of a bitlen-free limit, and two bit lengths $b,c$ give ceilings differing by at most $3\cdot 8^{-b}+3\cdot 8^{-c}$; at the measured bit lengths $48$ and $52$ the entire ladder moves by less than $10^{-40}$ at every depth. Confronting this with a six-cell measurement (bit length $\in\{48,52\}$ crossed with three seeds), all six cells lie in the band $[0.60,0.85]$, the dial beats a bare quadratic-residue count in every cell with exact mean advantages $+0.12$ and $+0.14$, and the measured mean drift $0.0036$ exceeds the entire geometric budget by more than $10^{37}$: the observed bit-length effect is sampling noise, not tie geometry. No cliff is geometrically possible, a worst-case linear-decline extrapolation stays in band to bit length $160$, and the advantage over the bare count is structural (cap $\rho^{2}<0.3829$ uniformly in $b\ge 6$). Finally, the rigidity is not vacuous: the $\ell$-adic ceiling $\frac{3\ell}{\ell^{2}+\ell+1}\bigl(1+\frac{1}{x(x+1)}\bigr)$, $x=\ell^{b}$, has a modulus-only prefactor that is strictly \emph{decreasing} in $\ell$, refuting the conjecture that finer grading raises the ceiling, and excluding every sampling modulus $\ell\ge 5$ at every bit length while $\ell\in\{2,3,4\}$ all clear the recorded value.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} Spearman rank correlation, tie correction, $2$-adic valuation, dyadic tie profile, Möbius rigidity, bitlen stability, quadratic residues, $\ell$-adic ceiling.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The problem}

An arithmetic statistic $T$ is proposed as a predictor of a target quantity, and its quality is reported as a rank correlation $\rho$ between $T$ and the target over a random sample of integers. Three validation questions arise, logically independent of one another: \emph{seed stability} (does $\rho$ reproduce across independent samples?), \emph{regime invariance} (does it survive a change of arithmetic regime?), and \emph{bit-length stability} (does it survive an increase in the size of the sampled integers?).

The third decides deployability, because the interesting arithmetic lives at larger sizes than one can conveniently instrument. It is also the hardest to settle empirically: a measurement at two sizes yields two numbers with error bars, and if the second is slightly smaller the data alone cannot distinguish graceful decline from noise, nor exclude a cliff at an untested size.

This paper settles the question structurally for the statistic we call the \emph{zero-fit dial}, whose tie structure is the $2$-adic valuation grading of the sample. We prove that the size of the integers enters the entire ceiling theory through a single scalar Möbius factor, so that the geometric bit-length effect across the measured range is below $10^{-40}$ --- thirty-seven orders of magnitude below the measured drift.

\subsection{Contributions}
\begin{itemize}
  \item The affine-shape (rigidity) lemma (\S\ref{sec:rigid}): every ceiling has the form $(Xg+h)/(X-1)$ in $X=8^{b}$, so $|\text{ceiling}-g|\le 3/X$.
  \item Closed forms for the three blinded ceilings (\S\ref{sec:ladder}), each with its bitlen-free limit.
  \item Bitlen stability (\S\ref{sec:rigid}): ceilings at two bit lengths differ by at most $3\cdot 8^{-b}+3\cdot 8^{-c}$; below $10^{-40}$ at $48$ vs.\ $52$.
  \item Noise attribution (\S\ref{sec:meas}): the recorded drift exceeds the geometric budget by more than $10^{37}$.
  \item Structural separation from the bare count (\S\ref{sec:sep}), uniform in the bit length.
  \item No cliff and no decline (\S\ref{sec:nocliff}).
  \item The live modulus axis and a refuted conjecture (\S\ref{sec:modulus}).
\end{itemize}

\section{Setup: tied rank statistics and the tie ceiling}

\subsection{Mid-ranks and tie profiles}

Let a sample of size $n$ be graded by a statistic $T$ into disjoint tie classes (level sets of $T$) of sizes $m_{1},\dots,m_{r}$ with $\sum_{i}m_{i}=n$. The multiset $P=(m_{1},\dots,m_{r})$, listed in decreasing order of the $T$-scale, is the \emph{tie profile} of $T$. The Spearman coefficient between $T$ and a second variable is the Pearson correlation of the two rank vectors, tied observations receiving the \emph{mid-rank}: every member of a class of size $m$ occupying positions $j+1,\dots,j+m$ receives $j+(m+1)/2$.

\begin{definition}[Resolved variance]
For a tie profile $P$ of total size $n$,
\[
V(P)\;=\;(n^{3}-n)\;-\;\sum_{m\in P}\bigl(m^{3}-m\bigr).
\]
\end{definition}

$V(P)$ is $12n$ times the variance of the mid-rank vector; $n^{3}-n$ is its value for a completely untied ranking, and each class of size $m$ subtracts the classical Spearman tie correction $m^{3}-m$.

\begin{theorem}[Tie ceiling]\label{thm:ceiling}
For any variable $Y$, the Spearman correlation between $T$ and $Y$ satisfies
\[
\rho^{2}\;\le\;\CC(P)\;:=\;\frac{V(P)}{n^{3}-n}\;=\;1-\frac{\sum_{m\in P}(m^{3}-m)}{n^{3}-n}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Correlation is bounded in absolute value by the ratio of the standard deviation of the mid-rank vector of $T$ to that of a fully resolved ranking of the same sample, the optimum being attained when the target is an increasing function of the mid-ranks with maximal spread. Squaring gives the variance ratio $V(P)/(n^{3}-n)$.
\end{proof}

Two features drive everything below. \emph{Ties cost cubically}: one class of size $1000$ is a thousand times more destructive than a thousand classes of size $10$. \emph{Merging is monotone}: since $(a+b)^{3}-(a+b)\ge (a^{3}-a)+(b^{3}-b)$ for $a,b\ge 0$, coarsening a response can only lower its ceiling.

\subsection{The dyadic profile}

\begin{definition}
For $b\ge 1$ the \emph{dyadic tie profile} is $A_{b}=(2^{b-1},2^{b-2},\dots,2,1,1)$, with $\sum A_{b}=2^{b}$.
\end{definition}

$A_{b}$ is exactly the profile of the $2$-adic valuation $v_{2}$ on $\{0,\dots,2^{b}-1\}$: the class $v_{2}=k$ has $2^{b-1-k}$ elements for $0\le k<b$, and $\{0\}$ is a singleton class. It is \emph{self-similar under bit-extension}: $A_{b+1}$ is $A_{b}$ with the entry $2^{b}$ prepended. This is the ultimate source of every rigidity statement below.

\begin{theorem}[Dyadic ceiling]\label{thm:dyadic}
For $b\ge 1$, with $x=2^{b}$,
\[
\CC(A_{b})\;=\;\frac{6}{7}\left(1+\frac{1}{x(x+1)}\right)\;>\;\frac{6}{7}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The cubes of the block sizes form the geometric series $8^{b-1}+\dots+8+1+1=\frac{x^{3}-1}{7}+1$, while $\sum_{m\in A_{b}}m=x$. Hence $V(A_{b})=x^{3}-x-\bigl(\frac{x^{3}-1}{7}+1-x\bigr)=\frac{6}{7}(x^{3}-1)$. Dividing by $x^{3}-x=x(x-1)(x+1)$ and cancelling $x-1$ from $x^{3}-1=(x-1)(x^{2}+x+1)$ gives $\frac{6}{7}\frac{x^{2}+x+1}{x(x+1)}$.
\end{proof}

Numerically $\CC(A_{3})=0.869048$, $\CC(A_{8})=0.8571559$, $\CC(A_{16})=0.85714286$: geometric convergence to $6/7$ with ratio $4$ per bit; at $b=47$ the correction is below $10^{-28}$.

\subsection{Relative ceilings and the bitlen parameter}

\begin{definition}[Relative ceiling]
For a profile $P$ obtained by merging classes of $A_{b}$, set $\Ceil(P)=V(P)/V(A_{b})$.
\end{definition}

Since $\CC(A_{b})<1$ we have $\rho^{2}\le \CC(P)=\Ceil(P)\cdot\CC(A_{b})\le \Ceil(P)$, so every bound proved for $\Ceil$ is a valid, slightly conservative bound on the attainable $\rho^{2}$. Throughout, $b$ denotes the number of valuation classes; a draw of exact bit length $\beta$ (leading bit fixed) has $b=\beta-1$, so the measured bit lengths $48$ and $52$ correspond to $b=47$ and $b=51$. We write $X=(2^{b})^{3}=8^{b}$; cubes are the natural unit because Theorem~\ref{thm:ceiling} consumes cubes.

\section{The ladder of blinded ceilings}\label{sec:ladder}

Fix a depth $t\ge 1$ and let $p=2^{-t}\in(0,\tfrac12]$ be the corresponding \emph{dyadic relation rate}: the fraction of the sample in the top $t$ valuation classes.

\begin{definition}
The \emph{coarse response} at rate $p$ reports one bit, whether the sample point lies in the top fraction $p$; its profile is $(n(1-p),\,np)$, $n=2^{b}$. The \emph{tip-blind response} at depth $t$ resolves the bottom $1-p$ perfectly and merges the top $p$ into one class. The \emph{bulk-blind response} at depth $t$ merges the bottom $1-p$ into one class and resolves the tip perfectly.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Coarse ceiling]\label{thm:coarse}
For $b\ge 1$, $\displaystyle \Ceil_{\mathrm{coarse}}(b,t)=\frac{7}{2}\,p(1-p)\cdot\frac{X}{X-1}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
With $x=2^{b}$, $V=x^{3}-x-[(x(1-p))^{3}-x(1-p)]-[(xp)^{3}-xp]=x^{3}\bigl(1-(1-p)^{3}-p^{3}\bigr)$, and $1-(1-p)^{3}-p^{3}=3p(1-p)$ because $a+b=1$ implies $1-a^{3}-b^{3}=3ab$. Dividing by $V(A_{b})=\frac{6}{7}(X-1)$ gives $\frac{7}{6}\cdot 3p(1-p)\frac{X}{X-1}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Tip-blind ceiling]\label{thm:tip}
For $t\le b$, $\displaystyle \Ceil_{\mathrm{tip}}(b,t)=\frac{X(1-p^{3})}{X-1}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The surviving classes $2^{b-1},\dots,2^{b-t}$ contribute the geometric cube sum $(X-(xp)^{3})/7$, and the merged block has size $xp=2^{b-t}$; assembling gives $V=\frac{6}{7}\bigl(X-(xp)^{3}\bigr)$ with $(xp)^{3}=Xp^{3}$. Divide by $\frac{6}{7}(X-1)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Bulk-blind ceiling]\label{thm:bulk}
For $t\le b$, $\displaystyle \Ceil_{\mathrm{bulk}}(b,t)=\frac{X\bigl(\frac{7}{2}p(1-p)+p^{3}\bigr)-1}{X-1}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The merged bottom contributes $(x(1-p))^{3}-x(1-p)$ and the surviving tip is a scaled dyadic profile on $xp$ points, contributing $\frac{(xp)^{3}-1}{7}+1-xp$; hence $V=X-X(1-p)^{3}-\frac{Xp^{3}-1}{7}-1$. The identity $\frac{7}{6}\bigl(1-(1-p)^{3}-\frac{p^{3}}{7}\bigr)=\frac{7}{2}p(1-p)+p^{3}$ finishes the computation, leaving the numerator constant $-1$.
\end{proof}

We write $g_{\mathrm{coarse}}(t)=\frac{7}{2}p(1-p)$, $g_{\mathrm{tip}}(t)=1-p^{3}$, $g_{\mathrm{bulk}}(t)=\frac{7}{2}p(1-p)+p^{3}$ for the bitlen-free limits. The coarse limit is the \emph{rate parabola}, maximal ($7/8$) at $p=1/2$: a single output bit retains at most a parabola's worth of rank information. The tip limit is close to $1$ even at modest depth ($0.998$ at $t=3$): blinding the tip is cheap because the tip is small and ties cost cubically.

\begin{lemma}[Limits lie in $[0,1]$]\label{lem:limits}
For every $t\ge 1$ the three limits lie in $[0,1]$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\frac{7}{2}p(1-p)\le \frac{7}{8}$ and $0\le p^{3}\le 1$ dispose of the first two. For the third,
\[
1-\Bigl(\tfrac{7}{2}p(1-p)+p^{3}\Bigr)=\tfrac{1}{2}(1-2p)(p-1)(p-2),
\]
whose factors are $\ge 0$ (as $p\le\frac12$ for $t\ge1$), $\le 0$ and $\le 0$, so the product is $\ge 0$. Equality holds at $t=1$, where $g_{\mathrm{bulk}}=1$; the bound $g\le 1$ is therefore attained.
\end{proof}

\section{Möbius rigidity}\label{sec:rigid}

\begin{theorem}[Affine-shape bound]\label{thm:shape}
Let $X\ge 8$, $g\in[0,1]$, $|h|\le 1$. Then
\[
\left|\frac{Xg+h}{X-1}-g\right|\;\le\;\frac{3}{X}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\frac{Xg+h}{X-1}-g=\frac{Xg+h-g(X-1)}{X-1}=\frac{g+h}{X-1}$, and $|g+h|\le 2$; finally $2/(X-1)\le 3/X$ is equivalent to $X\ge 3$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Shape of the ladder]\label{cor:shape}
With $X=8^{b}$,
\[
\Ceil_{\mathrm{coarse}}(b,t)=\frac{Xg_{\mathrm{coarse}}(t)+0}{X-1},\qquad
\Ceil_{\mathrm{tip}}(b,t)=\frac{Xg_{\mathrm{tip}}(t)+0}{X-1},\qquad
\Ceil_{\mathrm{bulk}}(b,t)=\frac{Xg_{\mathrm{bulk}}(t)-1}{X-1},
\]
with bitlen-free $g_{\bullet}(t)\in[0,1]$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:limits} and $h\in\{0,-1\}$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Closeness]\label{cor:close}
For $b\ge 1$ and $1\le t\le b$, $\;\bigl|\Ceil_{\bullet}(b,t)-g_{\bullet}(t)\bigr|\le 3\cdot 8^{-b}$ for each of the three ceilings.
\end{corollary}

Thus bit length acts on the whole theory only through the Möbius factor $\frac{X}{X-1}=1+\frac{1}{X-1}$, which equals $1+7.3\times 10^{-12}$ already at $b=12$.

\begin{theorem}[Ladder bitlen stability]\label{thm:stable}
For $b,c\ge 1$ and $1\le t\le\min(b,c)$,
\[
\bigl|\Ceil_{\bullet}(b,t)-\Ceil_{\bullet}(c,t)\bigr|\;\le\;\frac{3}{8^{b}}+\frac{3}{8^{c}}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Triangle inequality through the common limit $g_{\bullet}(t)$, using Corollary~\ref{cor:close} twice.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Indistinguishability at bit lengths $48$ and $52$]\label{thm:4852}
For every depth $1\le t\le 47$,
\[
\bigl|\Ceil_{\bullet}(47,t)-\Ceil_{\bullet}(51,t)\bigr|\;\le\;\frac{3}{8^{47}}+\frac{3}{8^{51}}\;\approx\;1.08\times 10^{-42}\;<\;10^{-40}.
\]
\end{theorem}

No measurement at any realistic precision can resolve a bit-length effect of this size: whatever is observed between bit lengths $48$ and $52$ is not tie geometry.

\section{The measurement and its attribution}\label{sec:meas}

The experiment crosses two bit lengths with three independent seeds, recording the dial's rank correlation and, as a control, that of a \emph{bare quadratic-residue count} --- a single-bit response reporting only a residue-count threshold.

